WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.067 --> 00:00:05.900
JENNIFER DANIEL: Hi.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:07.310
My name's Jennifer Daniel.

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:09.110
I'm the Expressions
Creative Director,

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:12.440
and I'm going to talk to you
about how we encode gender

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:17.360
into technical
artifacts, such as emoji.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.400
Working on Google's
emoji program

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:25.080
has a large breadth of
interesting aspects of my job.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:29.090
It includes reviewing emoji
proposals, designing emojis,

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:31.040
and the experience using them.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.520
It means working with teams all
across Google, like the Fonts

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:38.180
team to innovate on the font
technology, or with Gboard

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:40.880
to improve search
prediction and suggestion.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:43.370
If you're running the latest
software on your phone,

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:47.420
there's over 3,000 emojis.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:53.170
And each one of those goes
through a rigorous process

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:55.600
to really land on your keyboard.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:58.130
I want to talk quickly
about one proposal that

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:03.434
came across our desk, not too
long ago, for a knot emoji.

00:01:03.434 --> 00:01:04.849
Ta-da!

00:01:04.849 --> 00:01:06.199
Knot.

00:01:06.199 --> 00:01:08.320
A knot emoji.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:10.640
OK, sure.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:13.400
When reviewing proposal,
a large part of my job

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:16.730
is to anticipate,
is this something

00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:20.810
that people are already using
that warrants emojification?

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:24.680
Is this something that
will be frequently used?

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:29.350
And the proposal talks
about some fairly reasonable

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:34.450
examples, like people
tie knots when they sail.

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:36.670
The knot is an
often used metaphor

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:41.330
to talk about marriage,
like tying the knot.

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:44.740
However, what the
proposal doesn't get into

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:48.960
is how the knot has become
emblematic of other things.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:52.540
For example, when paired with
an emoji, such as a tree,

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:56.420
it can communicate something
like lynching or suicide.

00:01:56.420 --> 00:02:00.980
My job as a designer is to, not
just anticipate those problems,

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:03.980
but demonstrate how
design can truly change

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:06.050
the function of something.

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:08.930
Like creating a knot
that's more colorful

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:10.639
or a different
style of knot that

00:02:10.639 --> 00:02:14.300
has less negative connotations.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:19.160
My job in emoji didn't start
to long ago, maybe about 2018

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:20.230
or so.

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:23.600
And it didn't start
with this, but this

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:26.570
is when I really
started investigating

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:30.930
how we encode gender
into technical artifacts,

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:32.390
such as emoji.

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:35.910
And it was when I texted
a friend in a meeting.

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:39.770
Love a good mansplain,
woman face-palming.

00:02:39.770 --> 00:02:44.540
And she, on her phone,
received love a good mansplain,

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:48.120
but with a man face-palming.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:50.070
Now even though
both of these emojis

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:53.100
are technically the same since
they map to the same code

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:58.080
point, they were rendering
in different genders.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:01.320
And this creates all kinds of
cross platform inconsistencies,

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:02.340
like--

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:06.060
"Oh, this is an image for
a person in steamy room.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:07.680
What is that man doing there?

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:08.970
I do not need to see him.

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:09.930
I am alone.

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:12.690
I'm trying to be peaceful
in my steam room."

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:15.890
It turns out, this
happens a number of times.

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:19.100
I send you a merperson
from my Pixel device,

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:21.920
and you get a merman
on your iPhone.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:25.100
You send me a dead
zombie on your iPhone,

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:28.860
and I get a dead lady
on my Android device.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:30.300
So what's happening here?

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:33.430
Well, basically, vendors
such as Apple, and Facebook,

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:37.670
and Google, and Samsung,
have given gendered designs

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:41.930
for emojis that aren't supposed
to be explicitly gendered.

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:44.450
And this creates a number
of problems when there

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:46.270
aren't even inconsistencies.

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:48.760
For example,
construction worker,

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:51.300
which is not supposed
to be male or female,

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:54.230
everyone has designed
to be male, reinforcing

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:57.520
a stereotype unnecessarily.

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:01.510
Or police officer, not
policemen not police women,

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:04.890
again, everyone's
designed as male.

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:09.080
However, once you get to
the more emotional gestures,

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:11.450
they all default to female.

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:17.490
Person pouting, person
frowning, and person shrugging

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:19.350
are all women.

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:23.740
And this is deeply
annoying and stupid.

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:26.840
So how did we get here?

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:30.190
Well, first, you must understand
that emoji are part of Unicode.

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:31.390
What is Unicode?

00:04:31.390 --> 00:04:34.810
They're a standards body that
encodes the world's languages.

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:37.750
Unicode, to put it
simply, says that here

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:39.710
are characters on a list.

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:41.200
This is what they mean.

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:42.820
This is what their names are.

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:45.160
And this is how they map
between legacy and codings

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:48.030
in a universal character set.

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:51.520
So Unicode has a subcommittee
to standardize these pictograms,

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:52.940
which I serve on.

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:55.510
And now, true to
emoji's name, emoji

00:04:55.510 --> 00:04:58.480
are defined by two
attributes: the picture

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:01.240
the user sees on the
screen, and second,

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:03.130
the code that defines it.

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:07.390
And Unicode's job is to create
that code and those guidelines.

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:10.810
However, Unicode only
makes recommendations.

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:14.890
They don't actually decide
what the letters or punctuation

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:15.760
really looks like.

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:17.720
That's up to font designers.

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:20.380
And so now, we
have this cacophony

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:22.420
of different saxophones.

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:24.190
You look at Apple's design.

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:28.870
They tend to design their
emojis very, very realistically,

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:31.450
perhaps as a holdover from
the company's early days

00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:33.300
in skeuomorphic design.

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:39.730
What's up emoji are effectively
cheap knockoffs of Apple's.

00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:42.790
Microsoft embraces
a more flat design,

00:05:42.790 --> 00:05:44.650
due to their font technology.

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:52.600
And Google's designs are
more cartoonish and squishy.

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:55.150
The moon emoji is probably
my favorite example

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:58.270
of how these different
design approaches can really

00:05:58.270 --> 00:06:01.480
change the meaning of an emoji.

00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:02.950
So this is the moon emoji.

00:06:02.950 --> 00:06:04.390
It's a crescent moon.

00:06:04.390 --> 00:06:06.220
Pretty self-explanatory.

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:08.380
But it's so much more than that.

00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:12.830
LG has a septum piercing.

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:17.200
Facebook's looks like
a little bit of cheese.

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:22.130
Microsoft reminds me
of a rotten banana,

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:23.585
and Twitter's is a toenail.

00:06:26.230 --> 00:06:28.360
Emoji design has
evolved quite a bit

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:31.630
from the '90s, when it
first became popular.

00:06:31.630 --> 00:06:35.260
This shift in creating designs
to show off retina screens

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:37.690
has really left a critical
part of what made emojis

00:06:37.690 --> 00:06:39.400
useful in the first place.

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:42.220
Emojis are no longer
letters and words.

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:44.170
The drink emoji is a
great example of it.

00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:48.780
The original drink emoji was
just the idea of a drink.

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:52.930
But now, as designers
sort of fetishize

00:06:52.930 --> 00:06:57.250
the design of what a
Martini looks like,

00:06:57.250 --> 00:06:59.380
it is no longer a drink emoji.

00:06:59.380 --> 00:07:02.510
It's a dirty Martini,
one olive, straight up.

00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:06.070
It's a very, very
specific type of drink.

00:07:06.070 --> 00:07:10.120
And when you get more
specific and more detailed,

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:13.000
it begs the question, well
now we have a Martini.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:17.380
What about a glass of whiskey
or a tropical drink or a beer?

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:21.040
And over time sure
enough, we now

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:25.210
have a full bar available
in our keyboards.

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:27.910
This isn't a trivial
new direction for emoji.

00:07:27.910 --> 00:07:30.340
When a design removes as
much detail as possible,

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:32.130
it effectively--

00:07:32.130 --> 00:07:34.330
it conveys the idea
of it rather than

00:07:34.330 --> 00:07:36.490
literally reproducing reality.

00:07:36.490 --> 00:07:37.990
So just going to
have a brief moment

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:40.690
of silence for the
original gender

00:07:40.690 --> 00:07:43.750
inclusive emoji, the blobs.

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:44.900
What are they?

00:07:44.900 --> 00:07:48.020
I do not know.

00:07:48.020 --> 00:07:50.640
Emojis really could have
gone in two directions.

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:53.900
In the early days, Android had
these little blob characters,

00:07:53.900 --> 00:08:02.840
which are neither animal nor
human, ageless, and broad.

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:07.600
And Apple went into a direction
that was a lot more specific.

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:10.900
The original emoji were
simple and abstract

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:14.320
and gender and race were
reasonably obscured.

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:18.850
However, in the hands of
some designers in California,

00:08:18.850 --> 00:08:20.770
these early design
decisions radically

00:08:20.770 --> 00:08:23.830
changed the trajectory
for Unicode and emojis.

00:08:23.830 --> 00:08:26.590
As emojis were designed with
more explicitly Caucasian

00:08:26.590 --> 00:08:28.570
and gender
signifiers, we started

00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:30.430
to see cracks in the system.

00:08:30.430 --> 00:08:33.309
Despite Unicode guidelines
to remain neutral,

00:08:33.309 --> 00:08:36.159
police officers
designed as a man,

00:08:36.159 --> 00:08:38.650
and when five skin tone
variants were encoded,

00:08:38.650 --> 00:08:42.640
Apple changed their default
from Caucasian to gold.

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:44.980
But gold is effectively
just another version

00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:47.670
of a cartoon white character.

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:52.590
And so here we are now, 2020,
opting for more detail, instead

00:08:52.590 --> 00:08:54.810
of less.

00:08:54.810 --> 00:08:56.880
And that's how our
adorable emojis

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:59.850
evolved from the
concept of a kiss

00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:01.920
to hetero-normative
representations of gender

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:03.360
and sexuality.

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:05.800
However, over the
past couple of years,

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:07.530
we've managed to
convince others to start

00:09:07.530 --> 00:09:10.080
to un-sex these emojis.

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:13.380
What's particularly interesting
to me here, is Google

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:14.830
made this mistake.

00:09:14.830 --> 00:09:16.240
And corrected it.

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:18.900
Then made the same mistake
again, and then tried

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:20.640
to correct it again.

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:26.130
It's just sometimes progress
is reversing legacy decisions.

00:09:26.130 --> 00:09:28.200
So now, there's this
Unicode document.

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:29.960
It's known as-- it's
like [INAUDIBLE]..

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:32.300
It's sort of like
the emoji Bible,

00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:35.990
and it provides guidance
to how to implement emoji.

00:09:35.990 --> 00:09:39.590
Regarding gender, it states
other than the above list shown

00:09:39.590 --> 00:09:41.990
here, human form
emoji should normally

00:09:41.990 --> 00:09:44.090
be depicted in a
gender neutral way,

00:09:44.090 --> 00:09:47.800
unless gender appearance
is explicitly specified.

00:09:47.800 --> 00:09:50.030
And that's all fine
and good, until you

00:09:50.030 --> 00:09:56.090
realize that as of Unicode
11.0, 63 of the 87 emoji

00:09:56.090 --> 00:09:58.280
were defined as
gender inclusive.

00:09:58.280 --> 00:10:01.580
But most vendors did
not have unique designs

00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:03.800
for these glyphs.

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:07.610
These are the only 11 emoji
that everyone supports

00:10:07.610 --> 00:10:09.770
in a gender ambiguous design.

00:10:09.770 --> 00:10:14.690
Child, adult, older person,
snowboarder, angel, baby,

00:10:14.690 --> 00:10:19.340
skier, person in bad, fencer,
person in bathtub, and horse

00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:20.510
racer.

00:10:20.510 --> 00:10:23.010
So let's take a look at
these guys for a second.

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:25.640
Most of these emojis are
unique, in that they blissfully

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:28.790
exist without male
or female partners.

00:10:28.790 --> 00:10:34.640
Meaning there's no baby girl
angel or man in bathtub.

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:40.760
It's just person in
bathtub and just angel.

00:10:40.760 --> 00:10:43.700
However, there are three
child, adult, an older person,

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:47.460
that do have male
and female partners.

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:50.210
And this is really interesting,
because it indicates

00:10:50.210 --> 00:10:51.860
that people are
trying to operate

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:54.860
within a spectrum of gender,
rather than subscribing

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:56.930
to a false binary.

00:10:56.930 --> 00:10:58.670
And that makes me optimistic.

00:10:58.670 --> 00:11:03.190
However, as of emoji
12.0 the only designs

00:11:03.190 --> 00:11:05.660
that Apple supported
were for those 11

00:11:05.660 --> 00:11:07.460
previously mentioned emoji.

00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:10.750
All the other emoji that was
supposed to be gender inclusive

00:11:10.750 --> 00:11:12.350
were not.

00:11:12.350 --> 00:11:14.300
This was true of Microsoft.

00:11:14.300 --> 00:11:16.680
This was true of Facebook.

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:19.240
This was true of Twitter.

00:11:19.240 --> 00:11:23.320
Samsung inexplicitly has also
supported gender inclusive

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:25.200
superhero and super villain.

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:27.670
I don't quite know
why, but I suspect

00:11:27.670 --> 00:11:30.340
it's because most
of their features

00:11:30.340 --> 00:11:32.440
were heavily obscured,
which made making

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:34.570
the design a little bit easier.

00:11:34.570 --> 00:11:37.360
Like a baby step.

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:38.950
Google wasn't too
far off either.

00:11:38.950 --> 00:11:41.380
Google did not
support most of these.

00:11:41.380 --> 00:11:43.960
And it did support
an additional few,

00:11:43.960 --> 00:11:46.520
focusing mostly on
families and couples.

00:11:46.520 --> 00:11:49.060
UX plays a large
role here as well.

00:11:49.060 --> 00:11:51.130
Apple's keyboard,
their emoji keyboard,

00:11:51.130 --> 00:11:54.730
reinforces this false
binary by only displaying

00:11:54.730 --> 00:11:56.950
male and female emoji.

00:11:56.950 --> 00:11:59.530
But Google's keyboard
also had problems,

00:11:59.530 --> 00:12:01.910
where they may have only
showed one single gender

00:12:01.910 --> 00:12:03.910
in the keyboard, but
because some of the designs

00:12:03.910 --> 00:12:06.420
were explicitly male
and others were female,

00:12:06.420 --> 00:12:10.150
there was this arbitrary
mishmash of genders.

00:12:10.150 --> 00:12:13.830
So how did we address
this poor implementation?

00:12:13.830 --> 00:12:16.320
The visual design
played a big role.

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:18.450
Some emojis simply
required a change

00:12:18.450 --> 00:12:20.940
in hairstyle or clothing color.

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:23.880
Others required more refinements
like accessories, props,

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:25.280
and body language.

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:29.730
And others really forced us
to look each other in the eye

00:12:29.730 --> 00:12:33.530
and think deeply about gender.

00:12:33.530 --> 00:12:36.770
Now many have argued that
these emojis representations

00:12:36.770 --> 00:12:39.470
don't offer us much visually.

00:12:39.470 --> 00:12:43.760
However, we can't avoid gender
anymore than culture can.

00:12:43.760 --> 00:12:46.160
And you have to stare
at it really in the face

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:47.107
to understand it.

00:12:47.107 --> 00:12:48.690
And so that's what
we're trying to do.

00:12:48.690 --> 00:12:50.960
We're trying to find what
are the signifiers that

00:12:50.960 --> 00:12:54.620
make something feel
either male or female

00:12:54.620 --> 00:12:57.440
or both male and female?

00:12:57.440 --> 00:13:00.200
Gender, after all,
it's a construct.

00:13:00.200 --> 00:13:02.060
So when exploring
signifiers that

00:13:02.060 --> 00:13:04.460
have potential to
communicate this ambiguity,

00:13:04.460 --> 00:13:07.550
it's important to remember
that gender lives dynamically

00:13:07.550 --> 00:13:08.440
on a spectrum.

00:13:08.440 --> 00:13:11.240
And there's no single
visual design solution

00:13:11.240 --> 00:13:14.780
and giving un-gendered emojis
a gender inclusive appearance

00:13:14.780 --> 00:13:17.150
really isn't trivial.

00:13:17.150 --> 00:13:18.890
Gender inclusive
designs are intended

00:13:18.890 --> 00:13:21.200
to represent a
person of any gender.

00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:24.080
You could argue that a
gender inclusive design works

00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:26.420
if the gender
inclusive artwork is

00:13:26.420 --> 00:13:29.210
truly ambiguous to the viewer.

00:13:29.210 --> 00:13:32.450
But, due to each
individual's own perception

00:13:32.450 --> 00:13:35.780
of ascribing gender,
it's possible some people

00:13:35.780 --> 00:13:38.660
look at the existing
male emoji designs

00:13:38.660 --> 00:13:41.180
and see a woman with short hair.

00:13:41.180 --> 00:13:43.460
Or look at the
female emoji design

00:13:43.460 --> 00:13:46.220
and see a man with long hair.

00:13:46.220 --> 00:13:47.720
So where does that leave us?

00:13:47.720 --> 00:13:50.360
Are there signifiers
that can provide clues

00:13:50.360 --> 00:13:51.950
which don't rely on the binary?

00:13:51.950 --> 00:13:54.980
Like haircut or clothing
or color or body language

00:13:54.980 --> 00:13:56.610
or other facial features?

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:59.120
What level of product detail
is too much or not enough?

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:02.330
Because emojis really
demand an instant read.

00:14:02.330 --> 00:14:05.510
Contextual clues do
influence perception.

00:14:05.510 --> 00:14:08.540
Professions associated with
one gender more than the other

00:14:08.540 --> 00:14:10.730
can impact your perception.

00:14:10.730 --> 00:14:12.750
But what about if you're dead?

00:14:12.750 --> 00:14:16.110
What's your gender if
you're a part of the undead?

00:14:16.110 --> 00:14:17.920
And that's kind of the point.

00:14:17.920 --> 00:14:19.390
Context is king with emoji.

00:14:19.390 --> 00:14:22.600
After all, emoji with
contextual elements

00:14:22.600 --> 00:14:25.935
are most effective in
clarifying our meaning, provided

00:14:25.935 --> 00:14:27.810
there isn't something
that's contradicting it

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:28.690
or conflicting it.

00:14:28.690 --> 00:14:30.520
That isn't present.

00:14:30.520 --> 00:14:32.530
The least misconstrued
smiley motives

00:14:32.530 --> 00:14:36.130
are frequently embellished with
popular interpretation aides,

00:14:36.130 --> 00:14:39.040
such as hearts or
tears or dominant

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:41.260
upturned or down-turned mouths.

00:14:41.260 --> 00:14:43.630
So while, yes,
hair is one factor,

00:14:43.630 --> 00:14:45.550
other things like
clothing and accessories

00:14:45.550 --> 00:14:47.410
can inform perception.

00:14:47.410 --> 00:14:50.200
Given how fluid and
dynamic language and gender

00:14:50.200 --> 00:14:53.530
presentation can be, it's not
inconceivable that regardless

00:14:53.530 --> 00:14:56.830
of haircut, shirt color,
accessories, other clues,

00:14:56.830 --> 00:14:59.560
gender inclusive emoji
could be repurposed

00:14:59.560 --> 00:15:00.680
in some other manner.

00:15:00.680 --> 00:15:06.810
Like prince, princess,
emperor, head of state.

00:15:06.810 --> 00:15:08.910
And because I work
at Google, I really

00:15:08.910 --> 00:15:12.540
wanted to understand how
Google search interprets images

00:15:12.540 --> 00:15:14.130
as it relates to gender.

00:15:14.130 --> 00:15:15.930
A researcher at Google
had been exploring

00:15:15.930 --> 00:15:19.010
what happens when you apply a
makeup classifier to an image,

00:15:19.010 --> 00:15:21.040
and which features
change in that image.

00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:24.480
So I took the image she
generated and just put them

00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:26.230
through Google image
search results.

00:15:26.230 --> 00:15:28.980
So you see this image
of a prototypical-- what

00:15:28.980 --> 00:15:32.760
you might call a man,
right, Google image

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:35.640
sent back possible
related search gentlemen.

00:15:35.640 --> 00:15:37.920
I was like, OK, Google
image search gets it.

00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:40.950
They get that this is
a picture of a man.

00:15:40.950 --> 00:15:45.360
So ran the next image through,
and it changed it to boy.

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:47.150
Super interesting.

00:15:47.150 --> 00:15:50.970
OK, so what happens when we
run the next image through?

00:15:50.970 --> 00:15:52.590
It shot back boy again.

00:15:52.590 --> 00:15:53.670
OK, no change there.

00:15:53.670 --> 00:15:55.570
Got all excited.

00:15:55.570 --> 00:15:59.150
Well, the fourth image
also reads back boy.

00:15:59.150 --> 00:16:01.070
Maybe this experiment
will get me nowhere.

00:16:01.070 --> 00:16:05.190
But I ran the fifth one
through, and it changed to girl.

00:16:05.190 --> 00:16:08.220
And the next one
also became girl.

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:12.470
The last one returned
models with bleached brows,

00:16:12.470 --> 00:16:14.540
so we're getting
somewhere interesting,

00:16:14.540 --> 00:16:17.240
As it relates to what the
makeup classifier is changing.

00:16:17.240 --> 00:16:20.150
But also that there is the
spectrum of interpretation,

00:16:20.150 --> 00:16:24.296
not just in gender,
but also in age.

00:16:24.296 --> 00:16:27.050
So tackling the first phase
of using gender inclusive

00:16:27.050 --> 00:16:30.200
designs for code points
which do not specify gender

00:16:30.200 --> 00:16:31.280
is pretty clear.

00:16:31.280 --> 00:16:36.260
Support existing code points
that do not have gender.

00:16:36.260 --> 00:16:41.310
So this was Google's
emoji font set in 2018.

00:16:41.310 --> 00:16:45.750
And here it is in 2019,
fully compliant to Unicode

00:16:45.750 --> 00:16:46.830
guidelines.

00:16:46.830 --> 00:16:52.060
So now, when you text
someone, "I feel so relaxed",

00:16:52.060 --> 00:16:53.630
you don't have to worry.

00:16:53.630 --> 00:16:56.800
You can feel relaxed.

00:16:56.800 --> 00:16:58.750
When we launched
these, the reception

00:16:58.750 --> 00:17:00.760
was enormously positive.

00:17:00.760 --> 00:17:02.800
People finally
feeling like they felt

00:17:02.800 --> 00:17:06.069
like they didn't have to choose
between two genders anytime

00:17:06.069 --> 00:17:11.359
they just wanted to
simply express some joy.

00:17:11.359 --> 00:17:12.890
Most of the gender
inclusive emoji

00:17:12.890 --> 00:17:16.230
rolled out in 2019,
although some rolled out

00:17:16.230 --> 00:17:17.640
in 2020 as well.

00:17:17.640 --> 00:17:19.740
And we're seeing
much wider adoption.

00:17:19.740 --> 00:17:23.089
There's been rapid development,
as major vendors such as Apple

00:17:23.089 --> 00:17:27.267
roll out their designs,
and Twitter as well.

00:17:27.267 --> 00:17:29.100
The experience around
selecting these emojis

00:17:29.100 --> 00:17:30.990
seems to be a work in progress.

00:17:30.990 --> 00:17:33.660
Apple has chosen to simply
add the third option

00:17:33.660 --> 00:17:37.400
alongside male and
female designs.

00:17:37.400 --> 00:17:40.340
Google has opted for one
design to be the default,

00:17:40.340 --> 00:17:42.800
asking people to opt
into their gender.

00:17:42.800 --> 00:17:44.450
This also makes
it really so much

00:17:44.450 --> 00:17:48.110
easier to just browse your
keyboard for the right emoji,

00:17:48.110 --> 00:17:50.390
because there's less redundancy.

00:17:50.390 --> 00:17:51.950
But now comes the hard part.

00:17:51.950 --> 00:17:56.010
What to do with the 12
explicitly gendered emoji?

00:17:56.010 --> 00:17:58.520
OK, man in tuxedo.

00:17:58.520 --> 00:18:00.660
This is fairly simple.

00:18:00.660 --> 00:18:02.240
We make it a person in tuxedo.

00:18:02.240 --> 00:18:05.720
We add support for a woman
in tuxedo and man in tuxedo.

00:18:05.720 --> 00:18:08.330
Now we have James Bond,
Jesse Bond, James Bond.

00:18:10.970 --> 00:18:16.490
That same logic can apply to
woman, person, and man in veil.

00:18:16.490 --> 00:18:23.292
But what do about Mrs.
Claus and Santa Claus?

00:18:23.292 --> 00:18:25.130
We get Mix Claus.

00:18:25.130 --> 00:18:26.980
Now I know what you're thinking.

00:18:26.980 --> 00:18:29.770
There's no such
thing as Mix Claus,

00:18:29.770 --> 00:18:31.750
but there's also no such
thing as Santa Claus.

00:18:34.680 --> 00:18:38.700
Now, the breastfeeding emoji
did stump the group a bit.

00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:41.660
Do we simply change the existing
design for this code point,

00:18:41.660 --> 00:18:45.170
to remove it from the
explicitly gendered list?

00:18:45.170 --> 00:18:48.620
And then we can, if so desired,
add additional sequences

00:18:48.620 --> 00:18:50.677
for men and women?

00:18:50.677 --> 00:18:52.760
Well the general consensus
was that people did not

00:18:52.760 --> 00:18:55.790
feel comfortable changing
the woman breastfeeding emoji

00:18:55.790 --> 00:18:58.420
to be more inclusive.

00:18:58.420 --> 00:19:00.310
And since we don't
use sex signifiers

00:19:00.310 --> 00:19:03.580
to denote gender
representation in our designs,

00:19:03.580 --> 00:19:06.850
we're really unable to settle
on a reasonable fallback,

00:19:06.850 --> 00:19:10.690
and opted instead to create
an entirely new emoji.

00:19:10.690 --> 00:19:13.630
Man, woman, and
person feeding baby.

00:19:13.630 --> 00:19:16.660
The logic being simply that
since the only emoji that

00:19:16.660 --> 00:19:19.690
depicts anyone one with
childcare was breastfeeding,

00:19:19.690 --> 00:19:24.040
there just really weren't
any emoji that really

00:19:24.040 --> 00:19:25.765
expressed nurturing your child.

00:19:28.460 --> 00:19:30.050
In my opinion,
though, parenthood

00:19:30.050 --> 00:19:33.770
is already representable with
a tornado and a pile of poop.

00:19:37.390 --> 00:19:40.750
And, people, one
of the things I do

00:19:40.750 --> 00:19:43.920
try to think about is how
this emoji is being used,

00:19:43.920 --> 00:19:44.920
and how it will be used.

00:19:44.920 --> 00:19:49.030
And so finally, we do have an
appropriate Father's Day emoji.

00:19:49.030 --> 00:19:50.890
Other emoji, like
bearded person,

00:19:50.890 --> 00:19:54.640
are clearly designed in a
way that's more masculine.

00:19:54.640 --> 00:19:57.130
Moving forward, bearded
person will be shown

00:19:57.130 --> 00:19:59.440
in a more inclusive manner.

00:19:59.440 --> 00:20:04.210
But what to do about bearded
woman and bearded man?

00:20:04.210 --> 00:20:06.730
Beards have long been
indiscriminate of gender

00:20:06.730 --> 00:20:09.110
throughout history,
and truth be told,

00:20:09.110 --> 00:20:12.850
this isn't really about women
with beards or men with beards.

00:20:12.850 --> 00:20:15.820
It's more of a question of
do people with long hair

00:20:15.820 --> 00:20:17.090
have beards?

00:20:17.090 --> 00:20:18.580
And the answer is yes.

00:20:18.580 --> 00:20:19.180
Right?

00:20:19.180 --> 00:20:21.040
When you watch
Eurovision, there's

00:20:21.040 --> 00:20:23.560
a great number of men
and women with long hair

00:20:23.560 --> 00:20:25.980
and beards in the world.

00:20:25.980 --> 00:20:28.140
And many that are very
cultural relevant.

00:20:30.730 --> 00:20:33.070
Which is why these
resolutions can be complex.

00:20:33.070 --> 00:20:36.400
We can't simply change the
design of existing emoji

00:20:36.400 --> 00:20:38.870
anytime culture changes.

00:20:38.870 --> 00:20:41.890
So we have to anticipate,
not just where we are now,

00:20:41.890 --> 00:20:43.690
but where we will be going.

00:20:43.690 --> 00:20:45.070
The flamenco dancer.

00:20:45.070 --> 00:20:46.370
She's iconic.

00:20:46.370 --> 00:20:48.790
She's really become something
emblematic of something

00:20:48.790 --> 00:20:52.560
so much more than
simply dancing.

00:20:52.560 --> 00:20:56.240
But if you really go back
and think about iconography,

00:20:56.240 --> 00:20:58.240
you can look at the
man bathroom symbol.

00:20:58.240 --> 00:21:02.910
Why does this mean man
and not just person?

00:21:02.910 --> 00:21:07.350
Because someone added a skirt
to create a woman design.

00:21:07.350 --> 00:21:08.620
Why would they do that?

00:21:08.620 --> 00:21:10.950
Because they've
effectively done is

00:21:10.950 --> 00:21:12.930
reinforced that,
what would have been

00:21:12.930 --> 00:21:15.780
a perfectly acceptable
default gender inclusive

00:21:15.780 --> 00:21:18.780
symbol for person, is now a man.

00:21:18.780 --> 00:21:23.270
Which reinforces our
man-centered society.

00:21:23.270 --> 00:21:24.720
I don't know what
the blobs were.

00:21:24.720 --> 00:21:27.330
They were just good
little friends.

00:21:27.330 --> 00:21:30.585
This was the original
dancer emoji in 2013.

00:21:30.585 --> 00:21:31.335
It wasn't a woman.

00:21:31.335 --> 00:21:32.235
It wasn't a man.

00:21:32.235 --> 00:21:33.300
It wasn't a child.

00:21:33.300 --> 00:21:34.450
It wasn't an animal.

00:21:34.450 --> 00:21:36.245
It was just a dancer.

00:21:39.140 --> 00:21:43.430
And 2015, phones get fancier.

00:21:43.430 --> 00:21:45.050
Details get added.

00:21:45.050 --> 00:21:49.580
And now, we have these
fairly sexualized dancers,

00:21:49.580 --> 00:21:53.610
and in their
specificity and detail,

00:21:53.610 --> 00:21:56.548
they've alienated a great
deal of other kinds of dance.

00:21:56.548 --> 00:21:57.590
What about African dance?

00:21:57.590 --> 00:21:58.640
Polynesian dancing?

00:21:58.640 --> 00:22:01.330
Or boogie woogie?

00:22:01.330 --> 00:22:07.730
And 2019, we see something
that is really fun.

00:22:07.730 --> 00:22:09.730
But when you're thinking
about encoding emoji

00:22:09.730 --> 00:22:13.990
and putting broad concepts that
cover the concept of dancing,

00:22:13.990 --> 00:22:16.540
not specifically
flamenco dancing,

00:22:16.540 --> 00:22:19.500
paying attention to those
details is really important.

00:22:19.500 --> 00:22:20.247
But I digress.

00:22:20.247 --> 00:22:20.830
What do we do?

00:22:20.830 --> 00:22:23.450
What do we do about woman
flamenco dancer and man disco

00:22:23.450 --> 00:22:23.950
dancer?

00:22:23.950 --> 00:22:25.390
We don't have
something that just

00:22:25.390 --> 00:22:28.310
conveys the concept of dancing.

00:22:28.310 --> 00:22:31.160
We are not going to change
the existing code points.

00:22:31.160 --> 00:22:34.480
This is not a path forward,
we want to move forward with.

00:22:34.480 --> 00:22:36.390
So what if we added
a third type of dance

00:22:36.390 --> 00:22:38.760
for the gender inclusive?

00:22:38.760 --> 00:22:40.650
This-- be provocative.

00:22:40.650 --> 00:22:42.040
Here's a break dancer.

00:22:42.040 --> 00:22:44.580
But it seems appealing.

00:22:44.580 --> 00:22:46.500
But is it future proof?

00:22:46.500 --> 00:22:51.530
Will we still be happy with this
gender inclusive type of dance

00:22:51.530 --> 00:22:54.780
five years from now, two years
from now, 10 years from now?

00:22:54.780 --> 00:22:58.350
Will this create more problems
in the future or less?

00:22:58.350 --> 00:23:03.420
In my mind, if we get gender
inclusive break dancing emoji,

00:23:03.420 --> 00:23:06.270
why don't we have it for for
women and for men as well?

00:23:08.950 --> 00:23:12.960
So another possibility
is to just simply design

00:23:12.960 --> 00:23:14.850
male and person
counterpoints points

00:23:14.850 --> 00:23:18.500
to the flamenco dancer and
women and gender inclusive

00:23:18.500 --> 00:23:20.820
counterpoints to
the disco dancer.

00:23:20.820 --> 00:23:25.170
But do we really need
this many flamenco dancers

00:23:25.170 --> 00:23:28.170
on our keyboard and
this mini disco dancers?

00:23:28.170 --> 00:23:28.740
Probably not.

00:23:31.670 --> 00:23:34.820
Another emoji that
is being discussed

00:23:34.820 --> 00:23:37.810
is the pregnant woman emoji.

00:23:37.810 --> 00:23:41.410
One sex doesn't really dictate
the capacity to care for--

00:23:41.410 --> 00:23:43.990
in the home or at
work, or in the market.

00:23:43.990 --> 00:23:47.500
So if you look towards
sex equality law, that

00:23:47.500 --> 00:23:50.360
aims to combat harmful
sex stereotypes,

00:23:50.360 --> 00:23:53.860
it has a lot of parallels with
how we're un-sexing pregnancy

00:23:53.860 --> 00:23:56.535
and how it could do the
same for parenthood.

00:23:56.535 --> 00:23:57.910
So let's take a
look at how we've

00:23:57.910 --> 00:24:00.010
un-sexed the pregnancy emoji.

00:24:00.010 --> 00:24:02.080
The British Medical
Association's guidance

00:24:02.080 --> 00:24:06.400
on inclusive language
advises people

00:24:06.400 --> 00:24:08.530
to use the term
"pregnant people",

00:24:08.530 --> 00:24:11.260
instead of a stereotype
term "pregnant women".

00:24:11.260 --> 00:24:12.042
Why?

00:24:12.042 --> 00:24:13.750
Because though a large
majority of people

00:24:13.750 --> 00:24:15.670
who have been pregnant
or have given birth,

00:24:15.670 --> 00:24:19.600
identify as women there are
intersex men and trans-men who

00:24:19.600 --> 00:24:22.350
may get pregnant.

00:24:22.350 --> 00:24:25.530
So we've decided,
instead of changing

00:24:25.530 --> 00:24:27.450
the existent
pregnant women emoji,

00:24:27.450 --> 00:24:30.930
to simply add a swollen
belly to a person

00:24:30.930 --> 00:24:34.120
design and a swollen
belly to a man design.

00:24:34.120 --> 00:24:36.120
That is very similar
to what we did

00:24:36.120 --> 00:24:38.670
with the bride and the groom.

00:24:38.670 --> 00:24:40.710
However, much like
the flamenco dancer--

00:24:40.710 --> 00:24:42.480
and much like the
flamenco dancer,

00:24:42.480 --> 00:24:46.050
there was a strong desire not
to correct the legacy decision

00:24:46.050 --> 00:24:48.460
to double down on
pregnant women.

00:24:48.460 --> 00:24:53.280
But now, we finally have that
emoji for when you're hungry.

00:24:53.280 --> 00:24:54.660
You have eaten that burrito.

00:24:54.660 --> 00:24:57.990
What emojis can you use to
employ about how full you are?

00:24:57.990 --> 00:25:01.700
Sure, a burrito
and a tongue emoji.

00:25:01.700 --> 00:25:02.820
But that's not really--

00:25:02.820 --> 00:25:05.310
that face looks more
like "I'm hungry",

00:25:05.310 --> 00:25:08.040
not that I ate too much.

00:25:08.040 --> 00:25:11.160
But now, with swollen
belly, you finally

00:25:11.160 --> 00:25:16.260
have the perfect
emoji for lunchtime.

00:25:16.260 --> 00:25:18.450
Language emerges
from human minds

00:25:18.450 --> 00:25:19.950
interacting with each other.

00:25:19.950 --> 00:25:21.810
It's visible in the
unstoppable change

00:25:21.810 --> 00:25:24.810
we see through slang and
jargon and the formation

00:25:24.810 --> 00:25:26.610
of new languages, like texting.

00:25:26.610 --> 00:25:29.310
And what I love
about this program

00:25:29.310 --> 00:25:32.160
is that it forces me to
zoom out of my tiny world

00:25:32.160 --> 00:25:34.490
and realize that we're in
the middle of the story.

00:25:34.490 --> 00:25:37.510
We're not at the beginning,
and we're not at the end.

00:25:37.510 --> 00:25:40.600
As Google and others roll out
more ambiguous and inclusive

00:25:40.600 --> 00:25:43.390
designs for code points
that don't specify gender,

00:25:43.390 --> 00:25:46.180
a significant
acknowledgment is happening.

00:25:46.180 --> 00:25:49.270
Our emoji are moving
away from heterosexuals

00:25:49.270 --> 00:25:54.070
as their default to here are
two people, here is a family.

00:25:54.070 --> 00:25:57.490
The beautiful thing about
language is how it evolves.

00:25:57.490 --> 00:26:00.010
Words don't any stand
still like formal writing.

00:26:00.010 --> 00:26:00.730
It's like water.

00:26:00.730 --> 00:26:01.750
It's fluid.

00:26:01.750 --> 00:26:07.260
And Unicode's waters are still,
but they are very, very deep.

00:26:07.260 --> 00:26:09.400
So I just want to say
thank you for watching.

00:26:09.400 --> 00:26:12.350
I've really enjoyed exploring
this with all of you,

00:26:12.350 --> 00:26:17.860
and have a great day.

